{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And they saw the Elohim of Israel.</b> The intent of this is concerning the lower Glory mentioned above, and therefore it added the sign of the accusative (את) in it, which is the Shekhinah, since YHWH, who is designated as the Elohim of Israel — of Him it is written (Exodus 33:20): ״כי לא יראני האדם וחי״. And therefore Onkelos translated: וחזו ית יקר אלהא דישראל — and they saw the glory of the Elohim of Israel. And this is the glory that Ezekiel saw (Ezekiel 1), ״כמראה אדם עליו מלמעלה״ — as the appearance of a man upon it from above — for he first saw the holy Ḥayyot and the Ophanim below, beside the Ḥayyot, and he went back and saw the firmament stretched over the heads of the Ḥayyot, and he saw the throne upon the firmament, and upon the likeness of the throne, a likeness like the appearance of a man upon it from above." "<b>And under His feet was like the making of a brick of sapphire.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat), Scripture explains that Moses and Aaron and his sons and the seventy elders now saw, at the standing at Mount Sinai, the Elohim of Israel upon the throne, and the throne upon the firmament, and it is the firmament stretched over the heads of the Ḥayyot. He said first, ״and under His feet,״ which is the throne, and so is its Targum: כורסי יקריה — the throne of His glory. And he mentioned ״like the making of a brick of sapphire״ (לבנת הספיר), for so it is written (Ezekiel 1): ״כמראה אבן ספיר דמות כסא״ — as the appearance of a sapphire stone, the likeness of a throne. And sapphire is a precious white stone, according to the opinion of our master Saadia Gaon, of blessed memory; and therefore he said לבנת, from the root לובן (whiteness), and its meaning is great clarity, such that the light is seen through it." "<b>However,</b> the opinion of the sage R. Eliezer, of blessed memory, is that sapphire is tekhelet (blue-violet), and he said that לבנת is not from the root לובן (whiteness) but rather as in the language of (Ezekiel 4:1): ״ואתה קח לך לבנה״ — and you, take for yourself a brick. And he said לבנת הספיר, just as Ezekiel saw אבן ספיר — a sapphire stone. And the proof for his interpretation is that he said כמעשה — 'like the making of' — and did not say כמראה לבנת — 'like the appearance of a brick' — or כעין לבנה — 'like the likeness of a brick,' for regarding the hue of whiteness the expression כמעשה (like the making of) does not apply. And the meaning of the verse will be that they saw the throne of glory like the making of a brick of clear and bright sapphire, and beneath that throne, which is like the making of a brick of sapphire, they saw עצם השמים לטוהר — the very essence of the heavens in purity — and it is the firmament, like the appearance of dreadful ice, stretched over the heads of the Ḥayyot. And from this Ezekiel mentioned (Ezekiel 10): ״היא החיה אשר ראיתי״ — this is the Ḥayah that I saw, etc. — meaning, beneath the firmament, and the firmament beneath the throne, and the throne beneath the Elohim of Israel; but Scripture is abbreviated in this." "<b>And so</b> the Ramban, of blessed memory, wrote in his commentary, that Scripture took a concise path, for above the Ḥayah is the firmament, above the firmament the throne, above the throne the Elohim of Israel." "<b>And</b> that which he mentioned עצם השמים — the essence of the heavens — and did not mention the holy Ḥayyot that are beneath it, it is possible to explain that he wished to conceal them, because from there was the sin for Israel, that they cast down one of its טטרמולין, and thereby provoked Him." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: 'like the work of sapphire pavement' — in order to recall the enslavement of Israel in bricks. And the explanation of the matter is to teach that the Holy One, blessed be He, suffers pain when the nations cause Israel pain, and this is what they said: whoever enslaves them from below, it is as if he enslaves them from above. And from this matter is what is written (Exodus 3:7): ״כי ידעתי את מכאוביו״, and from this they said in the Jerusalem Talmud, tractate Sukkah: 'It is not written \"the work of\" but \"like the work of\" — it and all its fittings, the basket and the trowel,' end quote. And these seventy elders are the officers who were struck on behalf of the public to lighten from them the burden of the bricks, and therefore they were rewarded measure for measure to attain 'like the work of sapphire pavement.'" "<b>And by way of</b> reason (sekhel): 'like the work of sapphire pavement' — it is a grasp, in the vision of prophecy, of the truth of the first, lower matter (homer), which was from the snow that is beneath the Throne of Glory. And this is what the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded: 'The heavens — from what were they created? From the light of His garment, as is said (Psalms 104:2): ״עוטה אור כשלמה נוטה שמים כיריעה״. The earth was created from the snow that is beneath the Throne of Glory, as is said (Job 37:6): ״כי לשלג יאמר הוא ארץ״ and so on.' And Onkelos, who translated ותחות כורסי יקריה, rendered 'His feet' as resting upon the Throne. And the word רגליו means its causes (sibhotav), in the manner of (Ezekiel 1:7): ״ורגליהם רגל ישרה״, (Zechariah 14:4): ״ועמדו רגליו״; and the intention therein is that that matter emanates from Him, and He is its cause that necessitates its existence, and He created it and brought it into being anew after its nonexistence. And it says 'like the work of' because matter is acted upon and receives form; and it says לבנת ('brick') because that whiteness beneath the Throne was matter for the earth. And it did not say כלבנת הספיר ('like a sapphire brick') because the intention is not the appearance of whiteness — for sapphire is not white; rather, had it had the appearance of whiteness, it would not show all appearances and would not receive them from behind it; rather, it is devoid of every appearance, and therefore it receives every appearance. So too prime matter is devoid of all forms and has no likeness or form at all; therefore it receives each and every form. And this is the view of the Rambam, of blessed memory, in the Guide for the Perplexed, Part II, chapter 26, there." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: 'like the work of sapphire pavement' — it is known among the masters of the Chariot that the Throne of Glory is founded upon three elements — fire, wind (ruach), water — which emanate from three attributes: חסד (lovingkindness), דין (judgment), רחמים (mercy); and they are three appearances: white, red, blue (תכלת). And set it upon your heart that he mentioned לבנת for the appearance of whiteness, and ספיר for the appearance of red, and כעצם השמים for the appearance of blue (תכלת). And therefore Onkelos translated ותחות כורסי יקריה; and this is what is said: 'The תכלת resembles the sea, and the sea resembles the firmament, and the firmament resembles the Throne of Glory.'" "<b>And it is fitting</b> that you reflect upon how it mentions ותחת רגליו כמעשה לבנת הספיר, which is a pure clarification: that beneath the Throne there is something 'like the work of sapphire pavement,' which is another throne, and this indicates two thrones. And behold, the matter is true and established, for there is a throne for the throne; therefore the verse reveals to you explicitly that beneath the upper throne there is another throne, which is 'like the work of sapphire pavement' and 'like the essence of the heavens.' And three elements for each and every throne — behold, six (ו'); and this is the secret of the verse mentioned concerning Solomon's throne (I Kings 10:19): ״שש מעלות לכסא״ and so on; and it is written (I Chronicles 29:23): ״וישב שלמה על כסא ה׳״. For Solomon, in his great wisdom, wished to incorporate into his throne the attributes of the two thrones, concerning which it is said in a time of favor (Psalms 122:5): ״כי שמה ישבו כסאות למשפט כסאות לבית דוד״, and it is said in a time of anger (Daniel 7:9): ״עד די כרסון רמיו״." "<b>And there are those</b> who explained 'like the work of sapphire pavement' as follows: He likened the grasp of the emanated reality (atzilut) to the matter of white sapphire, which shows the appearance of what is inside it — that which is inward and hidden there. And this inner, hidden thing is seen from within the exterior, but its essence is not seen and not known for itself; and this is (Exodus 6:3): ״וארא אל אברהם״ and so on. For the inner, hidden thing appears to those who see as sapphire, but is not known to them; and therefore the verse explains its grasp with a very fine parable, from which the thing parabled may be understood. And this is the language of כמעשה with the כ of likeness, for the matter of the grasp among them — and among all the prophets, all of them, and also among Israel at Mount Sinai — is what is called 'face to face,' a force facing a force (כח לפנים מכח). And therefore it says כמעשה, in the manner of what white sapphire does: the inner, concealed thing is seen from within the exterior, but its essence is not seen, for it is hidden and concealed from grasp. And this is clear."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {4 "טטרמולין (tatarmolin/tatromolin) is a term found in rabbinic angelological tradition, apparently a class of angelic attendants; transliterated as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:04:08.091633845Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}